The syntax for the {} is {option A,option B} with the comma as a seperator of values
In this example, when we enter the sub folder with */ we search through the hidden files {. and then search through the normal files ,} (there is nothing after the comma, this is to indicate all files)

Now we find out the size of the file. 
We use the du command. This is an example.
    bandit5@bandit:~/inhere$ cd maybehere00
    bandit5@bandit:~/inhere/maybehere00$ ls
    -file1  -file2  -file3  spaces file1  spaces file2  spaces file3
    bandit5@bandit:~/inhere/maybehere00$ du ./-file2
    12      ./-file2
    bandit5@bandit:~/inhere/maybehere00$ du -b ./-file2
    9388    ./-file2
As the goal states the password is in a file that is 1033 bytes in size, we add the -b option to get the size of the file in bytes. 
To do this for all the files we are looking for, we use the | character.
The | character pastes the output of the previous command into the next. 
E.g
    bandit5@bandit:~/inhere$ file */{.,}* | du -b -a
    503     ./maybehere11/spaces file2
    8854    ./maybehere11/-file3
    3147    ./maybehere11/spaces file1
    8261    ./maybehere11/.file3
    2501    ./maybehere11/.file2
    452     ./maybehere11/.file1
    8845    ./maybehere11/spaces file3
    4559    ./maybehere11/-file2
    1211    ./maybehere11/-file1

Using both commands along with grep to only output what we are looking for.
    bandit5@bandit:~/inhere$ file */{.,}* | grep "ASCII" | du -b -a | grep 1033
